  {
    "@id": "https://centredesmusiciensdumonde.com/en/calendrier/georgian-polyphonies-echoes-of-sakartvelo/event_43913_0",
    "@type": [
      "http://schema.org/Event"
    ],
    "http://schema.org/url": [
      {
        "@id": "https://centredesmusiciensdumonde.com/en/calendrier/georgian-polyphonies-echoes-of-sakartvelo/"
      }
    ],
    "http://schema.org/image": [
      {
        "@id": "https://centredesmusiciensdumonde.com/wp-content/uploads/2025/05/Ori-Shalva.jpg"
      }
    ],
    "http://schema.org/location": [
      {
        "@id": "_:g4466520"
      }
    ],
    "http://www.w3.org/ns/prov#wasDerivedFrom": [
      {
        "@id": "https://centredesmusiciensdumonde.com/en/calendrier/georgian-polyphonies-echoes-of-sakartvelo/"
      }
    ],
    "http://schema.org/name": [
      {
        "@language": "en",
        "@value": "Georgian polyphonies - Echoes of Sakartvelo"
      }
    ],
    "http://schema.org/description": [
      {
        "@language": "en",
        "@value": "<p>Georgian polyphony is one of the oldest and most complex of singing traditions. Family singing plays an essential role, having enabled it to survive through centuries of invasions and occupations. For Shalva Makharashvili, Andrea Kuzmich and their sons Shalva-Lucas and Gabo, singing is as natural as breathing, rooted in family celebrations rather than stage performances.</p> <p>Ori Shalva carry on this tradition in Toronto, more than 9,000 km from Georgia. The authenticity of their music stems from years of dedication and a musical heritage handed down from generation to generation.</p> <p>Ori Shalva, also known as the Makharashvili family, is a unique musical ensemble. Formed in Toronto by Shalva Makharashvili and Andrea Kuzmich, both from different cultural and linguistic backgrounds, the group was born out of a shared passion for Georgian polyphony. For over twenty years, together with their children, they have been passing on and keeping alive this complex singing tradition, recognised by UNESCO as part of humanity&#8217;s intangible heritage. Their performances offer audiences a rare opportunity to appreciate a living tradition that is still largely unknown outside the Caucasus.</p> "
      }
    ],
